Agent: I looked through your inbox, Sent (outbox), and Drafts but didn’t find any email that clearly records a decision about which paint to use on canvas.

Would you like me to keep searching? Options:
- Search all folders (including archived/All Mail) for phrases like "paint on canvas", "use acrylic", "oil paint", or a sender name you remember.
- Search for attachments (images/PDFs) that might be the recommendation.
- Read/summarize your recent unread emails (this will mark them read).

If you want an immediate refresher (in case the email decision was informal or you just want to move forward), here’s a quick summary of common choices and my recommendation for a hobby canvas project:

- Acrylic (recommended for most beginners/hobby projects)
  - Pros: fast-drying, water-cleanup, flexible, widely available, many finishes, beginner-friendly.
  - Cons: dries quickly (less open time for blending), skill/mediums can address that.
  - Notes: use acrylic gesso-primed canvas or prime yourself; heavy-body acrylics give thicker texture.

- Oil
  - Pros: long working time for blending, rich colors, smooth finishes.
  - Cons: slow drying, requires solvents/ventilation, more setup and cleanup, typically more expensive.
  - Notes: use oil-primed canvas or apply proper ground; allow long drying and varnishing times.

- Gouache / Watercolor
  - Typically used on paper. You can use them on primed canvas but they behave differently (matte, re-wettable for gouache). Not the usual first choice for canvas painting.

Quick practical tips
- Prime your canvas (gesso) if it's raw.
- For a beginner kit: a set of student-grade acrylics, a few brushes (flat, round, filbert), palette, jar for water, paper towels, and a varnish for finishing.
- If you want slow drying with acrylics, get a retarder medium or open acrylics.

Tell me which next step you want (re-run a broader email search, search by sender/phrase, scan attachments), or if you want help choosing specific paints/brands for your project and I’ll proceed.
